    28	#    cell, that crossing cell also becomes a new trigger. Example sketch:
    29	#    - Seed on a backslash blue segment emits a slash line.
    30	#    - That slash line hits an off-segment magenta relay, so a new backslash line appears.
    31	#    - Another seed emits a backslash line; the two families then cross and create fresh
    32	#      magenta trigger cells. Propagation alternates orientations until no unseen
    33	#      (cell, incoming-orientation) event remains.
    34	# b) Complexity calibration:
    35	#    Only two diagonal orientations exist, so the state space stays small and visually
    36	#    legible. The generator repeats the same alternation pattern across examples:
    37	#    seed -> relay -> crossing. This makes the cascade observable without introducing
    38	#    extra hidden categories.
    39	# c) Broken implicit assumptions from the original solver:
    40	#    - Triggers are no longer "only on blue diagonals".
    41	#    - A trigger is no longer one-shot; the same cell may need separate handling for the
    42	#      two incoming diagonal orientations.
    43	#    - Crossings are no longer passive overlap; they create new work.
    44	#    Therefore the solver must track orientation-tagged events, not a flat trigger list.
    45	# d) Placement legality:
    46	#    The generator keeps blue segments disjoint, keeps all chosen trigger/relay cells
    47	#    in-bounds, and rejects layouts whose required intersections land on occupied cells.
    48	# e) Visual observability:
    49	#    Every generated sample contains at least two initial blue diagonals, two off-diagonal
    50	#    magenta relays, and at least one fresh crossing trigger. The output therefore shows
    51	#    more magenta structure than the one-step original, and the extra structure is tied to
    52	#    visible ray interactions rather than to invisible color ordering.
    53	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    54	#    Segment paths are kept disjoint and relay/crossing cells are forced away from segment
    55	#    cells when possible. This avoids ambiguous blue/magenta stacking on the same local
    56	#    area and keeps the network sparse enough to read by eye.
    57	# g) New solver assumptions checked against the generator:
    58	#    - There is still at least one diagonal-aligned blue pair; generator always creates
    59	#      exactly two main segments with color 1.
    60	#    - Non-blue cells intended to matter use a single trigger color 6; generator enforces
    61	#      that and keeps the background different from 1 and 6.
    62	#    - Useful cascades require opposite-orientation rays and later relays; generator
    63	#      explicitly constructs both and rejects samples whose iterative result matches the
    64	#      one-step baseline.
    65	
    66	
    67	LINE_COLOR = 1
    68	TRIGGER_COLOR = 6
    69	
    70	H_RANGE = (11, 22)
    71	W_RANGE = (11, 22)
    72	SEG_LEN_RANGE = (5, 11)
    73	GEN_RETRIES = 500
    74	SEGMENT_PLACE_RETRIES = 120
    75	
    76	
    77	Coord = tuple[int, int]
    78	Event = tuple[int, int, int, int]  # r, c, incoming_orientation, color
