   289	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   290	    """
   291	    Complete the blue diagonals, then let magenta triggers keep spawning perpendicular diagonal rays until the network stabilizes.
   292	
   293	    Input structure:
   294	    - The background is the dominant color. Blue pixels still appear as diagonal endpoint pairs that define one or more hidden 45-degree segments.
   295	    - Magenta pixels can sit on those blue diagonals as seed triggers, and additional magenta pixels may appear off the diagonals as relays waiting to be hit later.
   296	
   297	    Rule:
   298	    - Fill each blue segment between its extreme same-diagonal blue endpoints.
   299	    - Every non-blue pixel touched by a filled blue segment emits magenta rays along the perpendicular diagonal.
   300	    - If a magenta ray passes through another magenta pixel, that pixel also fires and emits along the opposite diagonal orientation.
   301	    - If opposite-orientation magenta rays cross, the crossing cell becomes a fresh magenta trigger as well.
   302	    - Repeat this propagation until no new trigger-orientation event remains.
   303	
   304	    Key insight:
   305	    - The important state is not just "which magenta pixels exist" but "which diagonal orientation reached each magenta cell". The cascade alternates between the two diagonal families and must be simulated to closure.
   306	    """
   307	
   308	    out, _metrics = _simulate(grid, iterative=True)
   309	    return out
